@[expose] public section

/-!
# Ellipsoids: the representation

The definition is [1, Def. 4], CORA's `ellipsoid(Q, q)`: a shape matrix `Q` and a center `q`,
stored as CORA stores them, and denoting `E = {q + Q^(1/2) u | ‖u‖₂ ≤ 1}`, the affine image of
the Euclidean unit ball under the symmetric square root `G = Q^(1/2)` (Mathlib's `CFC.sqrt`).
That `Q` is positive semi-definite is a field, as CORA's constructor refuses any other: without
it the square root has no meaning and no operation could be exact. `Theorems/ConstructEqPinv`
shows the set is CORA's own description, `{x | (x - q)ᵀ Q⁺ (x - q) ≤ 1, x - q ∈ range Q}`.

The square root is what the definition is stated with, not what proofs go through: any `F` with
`F Fᵀ = Q`, of any width, describes the same set (`Theorems/ConstructEqOfFactor`), so a linear
image `M Q Mᵀ` is generated by `M G` and a sum `Q₁ + Q₂` by the two square roots side by side.
`‖·‖₂` is `CORALean.norm₂`, deliberately not `‖·‖`: `Vec ℝ n` carries the supremum norm.

## References

* [1] M. Wetzlinger, N. Kochdumper, S. Bak and M. Althoff. "Fully-Automated
      Verification of Linear Systems Using Reachability Analysis with Support
      Functions". HSCC, 2023, Article 5.
-/

